The storage and network teams have confirmed nothing of theirs is involved.\n- Fleet latency dashboard export with deployment annotations (telemetry): Fleet GET p99, hourly average of 5-minute p99 values (ms), night of 2–3 Nov. Annotation: 'gateway 5.8 rollout 22:05–04:10 (us-e 22:05–23:30, ap-s 23:35–01:50, eu-w 02:30–04:10)'. 20:00 90; 21:00 91; 22:00 93; 23:00 139; 00:00 284; 01:00 291; 02:00 296; 03:00 301; 04:00 298; 05:00 297. Daily median p99: 1 Nov 90, 2 Nov 91, 4 Nov 296, 6 Nov 294, 8 Nov 297. The shared change calendar for 2–3 Nov has one entry: gateway 5.8 rollout.\n- Gateway 5.8 release notes (approved 30 Oct) (change-record): Changes: (1) Fixes SEC-2291, a request-signature replay weakness reported by external auditors. Versions 5.7 and earlier remain exposed, and audit closure requires 5.8 across the fleet by 30 Nov. (2) Read path: parallel range-fetch fan-out raised from 4 to 8 for large objects. (3) TLS library upgrade. Known effect: gateway CPU +15–20% from the added signature checks. Rollback procedure: redeploy 5.7, about 4 hours across the fleet.\n- Interview notes, storage platform manager, 4 Nov (manager-account): 'We pushed a routine compaction tuning change through the fleet config system on the evening of 2 Nov, because backlog alerts had been firing since about 31 Oct. Fleet config pushes don't go on the shared change calendar and never have. Compaction is background work and yields to client reads, so it can't be behind a read-latency problem. And to be clear, we never pause compaction. Once backlog passes about 15% of extents, read amplification climbs and nodes start running toward disk-full.'\n- Network on-call handover note, 3 Nov 08:00 (staff-account): 'Elevated TCP retransmits on us-e spine-2 from 02:10 to 04:50 on 3 Nov, traced to a flapping optic. We replaced the optic at 04:50 and retransmits have been at baseline since. Nothing comparable in ap-s or eu-w. Gateway-to-storage round-trip time medians are 0.4–0.6 ms in all three regions, same as last week. We don't see a network angle on the GET latency issue.'\n- Customer success note and traffic summary, 4 Nov (document): On 30 Oct Orrin Media began migrating its archive into Tarnfield, writing about 18 TB/day across all three regions. It is a background copy with a 15 Dec deadline and is currently ahead of schedule. Orrin's contract allows temporary ingest rate limits with 24 hours' notice, and their technical lead has said pacing is fine. Fleet traffic, 28 Oct to 4 Nov: GET requests/s rose 6%, gradually across the week. PUT requests/s rose 14%.\n\nBRIEF:\nThe latency regression comes from the storage compaction tuning change (FC-7710), not from gateway 5.8. The change raised compaction threads from 2 to 6. Compaction's share of disk bytes rose from 38% to 71%. Disk use went from 41% to 97% within 5 minutes of the push, and node-side GET time rose from 61 ms to 262 ms. In every cell that received the change, p99 jumped within minutes, whatever gateway version it ran. The cell that skipped the change, aps-3, got gateway 5.8 and stayed at about 94 ms. A test rollback of euw-1 to 5.7 changed nothing. Network and traffic growth don't fit the timing. I'm fairly confident, around 90%. Don't roll back the gateway. Doing so would reopen the security hole and wouldn't fix latency. Don't pause compaction either, because backlog would grow toward disk-full. Instead, revert the tuning in one cell first and watch p99 and backlog, then extend to the other cells if it works. Consider a more moderate compaction setting afterwards. Hold off on limiting Orrin's ingest. The 10 Nov attribution job will show what drove the backlog, and the projection says backlog won't reach 15% before 13 Nov. Gateway CPU profiling is low priority.\n","numLines":20,"startLine":1,"totalLines":20}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BQrDwAN4toE2Aiukaw7tQb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
